<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Performance hydration principles">
  <div><strong>Start with context</strong><span>Duration, intensity, temperature, humidity, clothing and access to fluid all change the problem.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Sweat loss varies</strong><span>Sweat rate and sweat sodium concentration differ substantially between athletes and even between sessions.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Hydration is one input</strong><span>Fuel, cooling, pacing, sleep and training status also shape performance and recovery.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Performance first principles</p>
    <h2>Replace what matters without turning hydration into a rigid formula</h2>
    <p>Exercise increases metabolic heat production and can increase sweat losses, especially in hot conditions. Fluid availability matters because progressive body-water loss changes cardiovascular and thermoregulatory strain, but the performance effect is not identical in every sport or protocol.</p>
    <p>Research also shows that simple thresholds can be misleading. The impact of exercise-induced dehydration depends on the environment, exercise design, starting hydration, opportunity to drink and the athlete's ability to self-pace.</p>
    <p>A practical strategy therefore starts with the session: how long it lasts, how hard it is, how hot it is, what the athlete typically loses, and whether another session follows soon.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Build the plan</p>
  <h2>Before, during and after</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Before</h3><p>Arrive normally hydrated through routine drinking and meals. Aggressive pre-exercise hyperhydration is a specialized strategy with mixed context-dependent evidence and possible gastrointestinal trade-offs.</p></article>
    <article><h3>During</h3><p>Use thirst, planned access, sweat patterns, duration and conditions to guide intake. Longer or hotter sessions can make carbohydrate-electrolyte beverages more relevant than water alone.</p></article>
    <article><h3>After</h3><p>Restore fluid and sodium when meaningful sweat losses occurred, especially when recovery time is short. Rehydration is only one part of complete recovery nutrition.</p><a href="/pages/recovery-hydration">Recovery →</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where Best Hydrate may fit</p>
    <h2>A prepared carbohydrate-electrolyte drink within a broader plan</h2>
    <p>Best Hydrate adds glucose and listed electrolyte sources to water. That can make it a practical option when an athlete wants a prepared hydration beverage rather than plain water alone.</p>
    <p>It should not be presented as a universal performance enhancer. The evidence for carbohydrate-electrolyte drinks is strongest in contexts where exercise-associated fluid loss, longer duration, heat, limited food availability or rapid recovery make fluid, carbohydrate and electrolyte replacement more relevant.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Performance boundary</span><h3>Hydration product is not a complete fueling plan.</h3><p>Long endurance events may require substantially more carbohydrate and additional sodium planning than one hydration beverage provides.</p></aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Choose the use case</p>
  <h2>Different sessions, different priorities</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Endurance</h3><p>Long-duration work brings fluid, carbohydrate and electrolyte planning together.</p><a href="/pages/endurance-hydration">Endurance →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Heat & High Sweat</h3><p>Environmental heat and high sweat rates raise the cost of poor fluid access.</p><a href="/pages/heat-high-sweat">Heat & High Sweat →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Recovery</h3><p>Rehydration needs to fit beside refuelling, repair and rest.</p><a href="/pages/recovery-hydration">Recovery →</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

